0135 | This inequality is true as $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k} \right\rfloor<P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}$ and $\sqrt{P_k}-2>0$, i.e.~$P_k>4$. For $P_1=2$ and $P_2=3$ the RHS of (\ref{eqn8}) should be tested explicitly. In the first case we get $\left\lfloor 2+2\sqrt{2}\right\rfloor+2-2<3\sqrt{2}$, which simplifies to $4<4.243$. In the second case, we get $\left\lfloor 3+2\sqrt{3}\right\rfloor+2-3<3\sqrt{3}$, or $5<5.196$. Thus, (\ref{eqn8}) has been proven.\\
0136 | \\
0137 | We prove (\ref{eqn9}) analogously. The LHS inequality can be written as $\left(P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right)-\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor<1$ -- true on the basis of (\ref{eqn10}). The RHS inequality of (\ref{eqn9}) may be written as $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor<\left(P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right)+\left(\sqrt{P_k}-1\right)$. This is true seeing as $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor<P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}$ and $P_k>1$ for all $k$. This proves inequality (\ref{eqn9}).\\
0138 | \\
0139 | Thus equation (\ref{eqn7}) is true and Theorem \ref{Th1} holds for $y=P_k$ and $x=\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor+1$.\\
0140 | \\
0141 | Next, consider two intervals: $\left(P_k,P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right)$ and $\left(P_k,\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor+1\right)$. These two intervals are of the respective lengths: $P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}-P_k=2\sqrt{P_k}$ and $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor+1-P_k$. We will show that the difference of these lengths is less than one. Start from the obvious inequality $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor<P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}$, whence
0142 | we get $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor-P_k<2\sqrt{P_k}$, $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor+1-P_k<2\sqrt{P_k}+1$, $\left(\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor+1-P_k\right)-2\sqrt{P_k}<1$, as required.\\
0143 | \\
0144 | The above implies that both intervals contain the same primes. We also point out that the upper bound of the larger interval, $\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor+1$, cannot be a prime since it is an even number for any $k$.\\
0145 | \\
0146 | Now we need to prove that these intervals contain at least one prime. From (\ref{eqn2}) we find
0147 | \begin{eqnarray}
0148 | && \pi\left(\left\lfloor P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right\rfloor+1\right)-\pi\left(P_k\right)=\pi\left(P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right)-\pi\left(P_k\right)\geq\nonumber\\
0149 | && \pi\left(\frac{P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}-P_k}{\sqrt{P_k}}\right)=\pi(2)=1,\nonumber
0150 | \end{eqnarray}
0151 | which is precisely the first statement of the current theorem, inequality (\ref{eqn5}).
0152 | \item It is obvious that $P_{k+1}\in\left(P_k,P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}\right)$, since we just proved there is at least one prime in this interval. Hence, $P_k<P_{k+1}<P_k+2\sqrt{P_k}$. From the RHS inequality in the latest expression we get the second statement of the theorem, i.e.~an upper bound of the difference $d_k=P_{k+1}-P_k$ in the form of (\ref{eqn6}).
0153 | \end{enumerate}
0154 | \end{proof}
